WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.199
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online C one, Book two.

2
00:00:05.499 --> 00:00:09.153
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.453 --> 00:00:16.308
Unit five: Advanced Rhetoric. Today you will build sophisticated arguments with layered subordinate clauses.

4
00:00:16.608 --> 00:00:19.074
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:20.033 --> 00:00:23.452
Part one: words. You will learn ten new words.

6
00:00:24.317 --> 00:00:25.469
Syllogism.

7
00:00:26.483 --> 00:00:30.340
Syllogism means an argument with two premises leading to a conclusion.

8
00:00:30.950 --> 00:00:36.224
All humans are mortal; Socrates is human; so he is mortal: a classic syllogism.

9
00:00:36.817 --> 00:00:39.492
Your turn. Say: Syllogism.

10
00:00:42.333 --> 00:00:43.451
Syllogism.

11
00:00:44.217 --> 00:00:45.287
Anaphora.

12
00:00:46.283 --> 00:00:50.721
Anaphora means repetition of a word or phrase at the start of successive clauses.

13
00:00:51.317 --> 00:00:54.338
'We shall fight…, we shall fight…' is a famous anaphora.

14
00:00:54.950 --> 00:00:57.823
Your turn. Say: Anaphora.

15
00:01:00.667 --> 00:01:01.749
Anaphora.

16
00:01:02.483 --> 00:01:03.531
Hyperbole.

17
00:01:04.517 --> 00:01:07.677
Hyperbole means deliberate exaggeration for effect.

18
00:01:08.283 --> 00:01:10.759
'I've told you a million times' is hyperbole.

19
00:01:11.350 --> 00:01:14.176
Your turn. Say: Hyperbole.

20
00:01:17.033 --> 00:01:18.162
Hyperbole.

21
00:01:18.917 --> 00:01:20.336
Rhetorical question.

22
00:01:21.350 --> 00:01:25.544
Rhetorical question means a question asked for effect, not for an answer.

23
00:01:26.150 --> 00:01:29.545
'Who wouldn't want a fairer world?' is a rhetorical question.

24
00:01:30.150 --> 00:01:33.000
Your turn. Say: Rhetorical question.

25
00:01:35.833 --> 00:01:37.299
Rhetorical question.

26
00:01:38.050 --> 00:01:39.527
Persuasive structure.

27
00:01:40.517 --> 00:01:44.629
Persuasive structure means the order in which arguments are arranged to convince.

28
00:01:45.217 --> 00:01:48.691
Her persuasive structure saved the strongest point for last.

29
00:01:49.283 --> 00:01:52.841
Your turn. Say: Persuasive structure.

30
00:01:55.700 --> 00:01:57.363
Persuasive structure.

31
00:01:58.117 --> 00:01:59.071
Tone shift.

32
00:02:00.083 --> 00:02:04.115
Tone shift means a deliberate change in attitude within a text or speech.

33
00:02:04.717 --> 00:02:07.633
The tone shift from anger to hope moved the audience.

34
00:02:08.250 --> 00:02:11.158
Your turn. Say: Tone shift.

35
00:02:14.000 --> 00:02:15.129
Tone shift.

36
00:02:15.883 --> 00:02:17.105
Audience appeal.

37
00:02:18.117 --> 00:02:22.624
Audience appeal means the way a speaker connects with listeners' values or emotions.

38
00:02:23.217 --> 00:02:26.064
His audience appeal relied on shared memories.

39
00:02:26.650 --> 00:02:29.407
Your turn. Say: Audience appeal.

40
00:02:32.267 --> 00:02:33.755
Audience appeal.

41
00:02:34.517 --> 00:02:36.075
Discursive strategy.

42
00:02:37.083 --> 00:02:41.219
Discursive strategy means a deliberate way of using language to shape meaning.

43
00:02:41.817 --> 00:02:44.675
Repetition is a common discursive strategy.

44
00:02:45.283 --> 00:02:48.377
Your turn. Say: Discursive strategy.

45
00:02:51.233 --> 00:02:52.896
Discursive strategy.

46
00:02:53.650 --> 00:02:54.442
Diction.

47
00:02:55.450 --> 00:02:58.251
Diction means the choice of words in speech or writing.

48
00:02:58.850 --> 00:03:01.151
Formal diction suited the solemn occasion.

49
00:03:01.750 --> 00:03:04.205
Your turn. Say: Diction.

50
00:03:07.067 --> 00:03:07.940
Diction.

51
00:03:08.683 --> 00:03:09.673
Cadence.

52
00:03:10.683 --> 00:03:13.705
Cadence means the rhythm and rise and fall of speech.

53
00:03:14.317 --> 00:03:17.628
The speech's cadence built steadily to a climax.

54
00:03:18.217 --> 00:03:20.718
Your turn. Say: Cadence.

55
00:03:23.567 --> 00:03:24.533
Cadence.

56
00:03:25.333 --> 00:03:29.112
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

57
00:03:29.950 --> 00:03:34.692
Visal, you analysed a famous speech for homework. What made it so persuasive?

58
00:03:35.783 --> 00:03:43.797
Because the speaker built his argument as a clear syllogism, the audience accepted the conclusion before he even stated it.

59
00:03:44.883 --> 00:03:46.964
Was logic the only thing at work?

60
00:03:48.050 --> 00:03:56.832
Not at all. Although the logic was sound, it was the anaphora and the slow cadence that made people remember every line.

61
00:03:57.917 --> 00:04:00.206
Some critics call that manipulation.

62
00:04:01.317 --> 00:04:12.282
It can be, if rhetoric replaces evidence. However, when emotion is used to support a well-founded claim, I see it as legitimate persuasion.

63
00:04:13.433 --> 00:04:15.363
Part three: grammar.

64
00:04:16.217 --> 00:04:19.412
Grammar focus: Complex subordination.

65
00:04:19.712 --> 00:04:20.899
Look at the pattern.

66
00:04:21.583 --> 00:04:25.046
Because the syllogism was logical, the audience was persuaded.

67
00:04:25.950 --> 00:04:29.656
Although the logic was sound, it was the cadence that people remembered.

68
00:04:30.550 --> 00:04:35.533
When emotion is used to support a well-founded claim, persuasion becomes legitimate.

69
00:04:36.467 --> 00:04:46.967
C1 writers stack clauses to show how ideas depend on each other. Place the subordinate clause first for emphasis and follow it with a comma; avoid 'although … but' in the same sentence.

70
00:04:47.717 --> 00:04:51.832
Be careful! Don't say: Although the logic was sound, but it failed.

71
00:04:53.767 --> 00:04:56.451
Say: Although the logic was sound, it failed.

72
00:04:56.751 --> 00:04:58.472
Do not use 'but' after 'although'.

73
00:04:59.267 --> 00:05:01.977
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

74
00:05:02.767 --> 00:05:04.070
The Three Appeals.

75
00:05:04.370 --> 00:05:10.560
Since Aristotle, rhetoric has been described in terms of three appeals: logos, ethos and pathos.

76
00:05:11.567 --> 00:05:16.109
Logos persuades through reasoning, as when a speaker constructs a clear syllogism.

77
00:05:17.100 --> 00:05:22.617
Ethos depends on credibility, which is why speakers often begin by establishing their experience.

78
00:05:23.633 --> 00:05:28.640
Pathos, although frequently criticised, can make abstract arguments feel urgent and human.

79
00:05:29.633 --> 00:05:35.836
The most persuasive speakers combine all three, so that the audience is convinced intellectually and moved emotionally.

80
00:05:36.933 --> 00:05:40.854
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

81
00:05:41.717 --> 00:05:43.472
Choose the correct sentence.

82
00:05:43.772 --> 00:05:52.557
A, Although the logic was sound, but it failed.. B, Although the logic was sound, it failed to move people.. C, Although sound logic, it failed..

83
00:05:56.100 --> 00:06:00.155
The answer is B: Although the logic was sound, it failed to move people..

84
00:06:01.317 --> 00:06:05.093
'We will build, we will teach, we will heal' is an example of blank.

85
00:06:05.393 --> 00:06:09.696
A, hyperbole. B, syllogism. C, anaphora.

86
00:06:13.233 --> 00:06:15.500
The answer is C: anaphora.

87
00:06:16.650 --> 00:06:18.824
Which phrase means 'the choice of words'?

88
00:06:19.124 --> 00:06:22.766
A, diction. B, cadence. C, tone shift.

89
00:06:26.300 --> 00:06:27.974
The answer is A: diction.

90
00:06:29.117 --> 00:06:32.370
blank the evidence was weak, the speech still won applause.

91
00:06:32.670 --> 00:06:36.800
A, Even though. B, Despite. C, Because of.

92
00:06:40.333 --> 00:06:42.344
The answer is A: Even though.

93
00:06:43.483 --> 00:06:46.133
According to the reading, ethos depends on…

94
00:06:46.433 --> 00:06:50.493
A, emotion. B, credibility. C, statistics.

95
00:06:54.033 --> 00:06:56.439
The answer is B: credibility.

96
00:06:57.633 --> 00:07:00.599
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

97
00:07:01.450 --> 00:07:04.564
What rhetorical technique do you find most persuasive?

98
00:07:21.350 --> 00:07:22.804
Now listen to Visal.

99
00:07:23.104 --> 00:07:57.297
For me, the most persuasive technique is a well-chosen example that appeals to both logic and emotion. When a speaker supports a statistic with one real person's story, the audience understands the problem and also cares about it. For example, a campaign about road safety is far more powerful if it tells the story of a single family. Some would argue that this is emotional manipulation. However, as long as the example is true and representative, I believe it is honest persuasion.

100
00:07:57.597 --> 00:07:59.318
Excellent answer, Visal!

101
00:08:00.217 --> 00:08:04.587
Great work! Now you can use ten new words and complex subordination.

102
00:08:04.887 --> 00:08:09.037
Today's value is communication. Speak clearly and listen carefully.

103
00:08:09.337 --> 00:08:11.978
See you in Unit six. Goodbye!

104
00:08:12.278 --> 00:08:15.116
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

